  "topic": "Semaphore",
  "explanation": "A semaphore is a synchronization primitive used in concurrent programming to control access to shared resources by multiple threads or processes. It maintains a counter that can be incremented or decremented, allowing threads to wait when the counter is zero and proceed when it's greater than zero. This helps prevent race conditions and coordinate actions in multithreaded environments, serving as a versatile alternative to locks and condition variables.",
  "syntax": "sem_init(sem_t *sem, int pshared, unsigned int value); sem_wait(sem_t *sem); sem_post(sem_t *sem);",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <semaphore.h>",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    sem_t sem;",
    "    sem_init(&sem, 0, 1);  // Initialize semaphore with value 1",
    "    sem_wait(&sem);       // Wait (decrement the semaphore)",
    "    printf(\"Semaphore acquired\\n\");  // Perform action after acquiring",
    "    sem_post(&sem);       // Post (increment the semaphore)",
    "    sem_destroy(&sem);    // Clean up",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This code initializes a semaphore with an initial value of 1, waits on it to demonstrate acquiring the resource, prints a message to show the concept in action, and then posts it to release the resource. This illustrates how semaphores manage access and synchronization in a simple, single-threaded context, focusing solely on the core semaphore operations."
}
