Full Grok response: {
  "topic": "Semaphores",
  "explanation": "A semaphore is a synchronization primitive in C programming that manages access to shared resources by multiple threads or processes. It maintains a count to control how many threads can access the resource simultaneously, preventing race conditions and ensuring orderly execution in concurrent environments.",
  "syntax": "sem_init(sem_t *sem, int pshared, unsigned int value); sem_wait(sem_t *sem); sem_post(sem_t *sem);",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <stdlib.h>",
    "#include <pthread.h>",
    "#include <semaphore.h>",
    "",
    "sem_t semaphore;",
    "",
    "void* thread_function(void* arg) {",
    "    sem_wait(&semaphore);",
    "    printf(\"Thread accessed the resource.\\n\");",
    "    sem_post(&semaphore);",
    "    return NULL;",
    "}",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    sem_init(&semaphore, 0, 1);  // Initialize binary semaphore",
    "    pthread_t thread1, thread2;",
    "    pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, thread_function, NULL);",
    "    pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, thread_function, NULL);",
    "    pthread_join(thread1, NULL);",
    "    pthread_join(thread2, NULL);",
    "    sem_destroy(&semaphore);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This program initializes a binary semaphore to ensure mutual exclusion. Two threads are created, and each attempts to access the shared resource. The sem_wait call blocks the second thread until the first releases the semaphore with sem_post, demonstrating how semaphores synchronize access and prevent simultaneous execution in the critical section."
}
