  "topic": "Semaphores",
  "explanation": "A semaphore is a synchronization primitive in concurrent programming that manages access to shared resources by multiple threads or processes. It is used to control the flow of execution, prevent race conditions, and coordinate actions, offering simplicity and flexibility as an alternative to locks and condition variables.",
  "syntax": "int sem_init(sem_t *sem, int pshared, unsigned int value);",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdio.h>",
    "#include <stdlib.h>",
    "#include <pthread.h>",
    "#include <semaphore.h>",
    "",
    "sem_t semaphore;",
    "",
    "void *thread_function(void *arg) {",
    "    sem_wait(&semaphore);",
    "    printf(\"Thread is running\\n\");",
    "    sem_post(&semaphore);",
    "    return NULL;",
    "}",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    pthread_t thread;",
    "    sem_init(&semaphore, 0, 0);  // Initialize semaphore to 0",
    "    pthread_create(&thread, NULL, thread_function, NULL);",
    "    printf(\"Main is posting to semaphore\\n\");",
    "    sem_post(&semaphore);  // Signal the semaphore to allow thread to run",
    "    pthread_join(thread, NULL);",
    "    sem_destroy(&semaphore);",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This example shows a semaphore synchronizing two threads: the main thread initializes the semaphore to 0, creates a new thread, and then signals the semaphore. The new thread waits on the semaphore before printing a message and signaling back, demonstrating how semaphores control access and order execution in a concurrent program."
}
